  incident? "Debug production issues" for a browser app is the hard case — a user's [38;5;153mconsole.log[39m output is invisible to
  you unless something ships it off their machine.

  - [1mConsole only (structured).[0m Zero dependencies, no network, no privacy surface. But for the webapp it doesn't actually
    answer your question — you'd still be asking users to paste their console. Genuinely useful for [38;5;153msrc/[39m if that runs
    on a box whose stdout you collect.
  - [1mConsole + pluggable remote sink.[0m Same structured core, plus a sink that buffers records and batch-POSTs them to an
    endpoint you configure (flushing on [38;5;153mpagehide[39m so you don't lose the crash that matters). This is the one that
    actually lets you debug production. Costs: you need an endpoint to receive them, and retry/buffering/PII handling
    become real design surface.
  - [1mThird-party SDK (Sentry or similar).[0m Best out-of-the-box: stack traces, release tagging, grouping, a UI. Costs a
    dependency and a vendor in a repo that currently has zero of both, and gives you less control over what leaves the
    browser.

  What's expensive to change later is the [1mrecord shape[0m and the [1msink interface[0m — everything calls those, and changing the
  record shape invalidates whatever you've already collected. What's cheap is [3mwhich[0m sinks are switched on. So if the
  sink interface is right, adding a vendor later is a small change, and starting console-only and adding remote later is
  also small.

  [1mMy recommendation: console + pluggable remote sink.[0m It's the only option that solves the stated problem, it keeps the
  repo dependency-free, and putting a vendor SDK behind the same sink interface later stays a one-file change.
