[1m[38;5;231mWhere should log records end up?[0m

[38;5;153m❯[39m [38;5;246m1.[39m [38;5;153mConsole + remote sink (Recommended)[39m
     [38;5;246mStructured core with a sink interface. Console sink always on; batching remote sink enabled in production. No [39m
     [38;5;246mdependencies. Actually lets you see browser-side failures.[39m
  [38;5;246m2.[39m Console only, structured
     [38;5;246mStructured records to console/stdout, no network. Simplest and cheapest, but browser logs stay on the user's [39m
     [38;5;246mmachine.[39m
  [38;5;246m3.[39m Third-party SDK (Sentry etc.)
     [38;5;246mAdopt an error-tracking vendor. Best tooling out of the box; adds the repo's first dependency and sends data to a [39m
